## Solution

We wish to compute, for any real number $y$,

$$F(y) = \int_0^\infty e^{-x}\,\frac{\sin(xy)}{x}\,dx.$$

(The integrand's only free parameter after integration is $y$, so we write the result as a function of $y$.)

### Step 1. Justifying differentiation under the integral sign

Define
$$I(y) = \int_0^\infty e^{-x}\,\frac{\sin(xy)}{x}\,dx.$$

Formally differentiating with respect to $y$ gives
$$I'(y) = \int_0^\infty e^{-x}\cos(xy)\,dx.$$

To justify this, note that for every $y$ in any bounded interval $[-M,M]$, the partial derivative $\partial_y\!\left[e^{-x}\frac{\sin(xy)}{x}\right] = e^{-x}\cos(xy)$ is dominated by the integrable function $e^{-x}$ on $[0,\infty)$. By the dominated convergence theorem, $I$ is differentiable and
$$I'(y) = \int_0^\infty e^{-x}\cos(xy)\,dx.$$

### Step 2. Evaluating $I'(y)$

This is the real part of $\int_0^\infty e^{-x}e^{ixy}\,dx = \int_0^\infty e^{-(1-iy)x}\,dx$. Since $\operatorname{Re}(1-iy)=1>0$,

$$\int_0^\infty e^{-(1-iy)x}\,dx = \frac{1}{1-iy} = \frac{1+iy}{1+y^2}.$$

Taking the real part,
$$I'(y) = \frac{1}{1+y^2}.$$

### Step 3. Integrating back

Since $I'(y) = \frac{1}{1+y^2}$, we have
$$I(y) = \arctan(y) + C.$$

At $y=0$, the integrand $\sin(0)/x = 0$, so $I(0)=0$, giving $C=0$.

### Result

$$\boxed{F(y) = \int_0^\infty e^{-x}\,\frac{\sin(xy)}{x}\,dx = \arctan(y).}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
